                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Juneau AK",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued October 15 at 8:29PM AKDT until October 16 at 1:00PM AKDT by NWS Juneau AK",
                "description": "A stronger atmospheric river continues to move over the panhandle\nThursday evening, lasting through Friday morning. Multiple waves\nmoving up will bring persistent moderate to heavy rain for much of\nthe southern half of the panhandle, with the heaviest rain expected\nthrough Thursday night. Elevated gusty winds are expected alongside\nthis heavier rainfall, with the strongest winds expected Thursday\nnight. Prolonged periods of moderate to heavy rain will produce\nsignificant river rises with the potential for at least minor\nflooding. Gusty winds alongside the long duration heavy rainfall will\nbring a possibility of isolated landslides.\n\n* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...The following areas, Central Inner Channels, Southern Inner\nChannels and Southeast Gulf Coast. This includes the cities of\nPetersburg, Kake, Port Alexander, Craig, Klawock, Hydaburg, Coffman\nCove, Thorne Bay, And Wrangell.\n\n* WHEN...Through Friday afternoon.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, other low-lying areas and flood-prone locations.\nStreet flooding and flooding of creeks and rivers are possible.\nIsolated landslides are possible in areas of steep terrain.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Rain totals between early Thursday morning and late Friday\nmorning for the Central Panhandle and Prince of Wales Island area\nare still expected to be around 2 to 3.5 inches, with higher rain\ntotals favoring southern facing mountain sides. Highest rain\nrates are currently expected through Thursday night, where rates\ncould be as high as 0.3 inches per hour.\n\nWe will continue to see winds of of 20 to 30 mph with gusts to 40\nmph on Thursday, with potential for up to 45 mph wind gusts\nthrough Thursday night. With the multiple rounds of moderate to\nheavy rainfall and the elevated winds, there is possibility for\nisolated landslides along steep mountain slopes.",
                "instruction": "Stay tuned to further developments by listening to your local radio,\ntelevision, or NOAA Weather Radio for further information.\n\nYou should monitor later forecasts and be alert for possible Flood\nWarnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding should be prepared\nto take action should flooding develop.\n\nThe ongoing rains could swamp boats. Mariners should check their\nvessels immediately.\n\nAll residents on or near mountain slopes should be prepared for\npossible landslides.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
